Abstract
In order to better understand the adsorption of volatile halogenated anesthetics on metal organic frameworks (MOFs), sevoflurane vapor adsorption experiments were performed on commercial MOF-177 at different temperatures. Due to the surface homogeneity of such an adsorbent, arising from its almost unimodal pore size distribution and the absence of specific, coordinatively unsaturated adsorption active sites, sevoflurane adsorption isotherms exhibited a peculiar deviation from the Langmuirian behavior. Consequently, they show a “kink” at a specific pressure that increases with increasing equilibrium temperature. Successful modeling of such data by means of the TaluMeunier equation confirmed clustering of adsorbate molecules inside adsorbent micropores, similarly to water vapor adsorption on activated carbon, which may play an important role when designing a system using MOFs as the potential adsorbents for capturing anesthetics.
Keywords: Metal organic frameworks; Halogenated anesthetics; Adsorption; Isotherm modeling; Adsorbate molecules clustering; Medical waste gases
## 1. Introduction
Since the publication of the paper authored by Fox et al. in 1975 [1], more attention has been given to the adverse effects related to the uncontrolled discharge of halogenated general anesthesia gases (HGAGs) on human health and terrestrial environment [2-5]. Indeed, what characterizes these anesthetic vapors from other medications is that only little quantities of the administered doses are metabolized, while the excess is released into the outside environment, exposing non-hospitalized subjects to its harmful effects. Moreover, Cl-containing HGAGs (e.g., enflurane and isoflurane) are chemically classified as chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), thus constituting a serious threat to the stratospheric ozone layer, in addition to the global warming potential (GWP) of sevoflurane, desflurane and (again) isoflurane, which is three orders of magnitude higher compared to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ [6]. Among the convenient technologies for handling emissions of HGAGs, adsorption-based ones are recognized as the most effective [7]. The usual adsorbents to be employed in HGAG removal operations are microporous materials, which are defined by pore sizes $<2\mathrm{nm}$ . Among them, zeolites were used as first-generation adsorbents for capturing exhaled HGAGs [8, 9]. More recently, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have been shown to be very selective, thus being considered the high performance adsorbents for the removal of HGAGs [10-13] and also of non-halogenated anesthesia gases like xenon [14, 15]. In particular, chromiumbased MIL-101 MOF exhibits a peculiar adsorption behavior towards sevoflurane (SF): e.g., fully activated MIL-101 [i.e., with exposed coordinatively unsaturated sites (CUSs)] irreversibly interacts with SF molecules, causing damage to the adsorbent in terms of adsorption capacity. On the contrary, when MIL-101 adsorbs SF without CUSs being exposed, the absence of specific adsorption active sites (i.e., the occurrence of a more homogeneous adsorbent surface) promotes interactions whose mild intensity is adequate to ensure both a significant adsorption capacity and a full regeneration of the adsorbent [10]. On the basis of these findings, the present work aims to extend our understanding of SF adsorption on MOFs. For this purpose, the zinc-based MOF-177 was selected as a model adsorbent due to its surface homogeneity, mainly arising from an almost unimodal pore size distribution. Indeed, MOF-177 shows only two types of cages having very close diameters (i.e., 1.08 and $1.18\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively) [16]. Moreover, there is no presence of exposable CUSs inside MOF-177 structure [17]. SF adsorption on MOF-177 was then studied by performing adsorption isotherm measurements at different temperatures, as explained in detail below, and the analysis of equilibrium data by means of a suitable thermodynamic model allowed us to identify a specific mechanism governing the observed phenomena.
## 2. Materials and methods
Commercial MOF-177 powder (Basolite $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ Z377) was purchased from Aldrich. It was structurally characterized by means of X-ray diffraction (XRD) using a Philips X’Pert PRO apparatus (working radiation: CuKα). Textural characterization was carried out by N2 adsorption/desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ with a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 instrument. Commercial MOF-177 was degassed at 473.15 K for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ prior to such characterization. The specific surface area (SSA) was evaluated by means of both the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) and Langmuir methods, while total pore volume (TPV) was estimated using the Gurvitch's rule. As regards pore size distribution (PSD), we deliberately chose not to use an approach based on density functional theory (DFT). Indeed, using a DFT-based method is highly recommended if appropriate kernels (i.e., sets of isotherms representing adsorption in pores of different sizes at a given temperature) are available with respect to the specific adsorbent, adsorbate, and pore geometry. Unfortunately, the very specific surface properties of MOFs are a large obstacle to the development of a characteristic MOF DFT kernel [18]. Based on this, the DFT-based methods have been viewed as inappropriate for determining PSDs of MOFs, thus reverting to classical methods has been recommended, as long as no better alternatives are available [19]. As a result, we opted for the Horvath-Kawazoe (HK) method for evaluating the PSD of commercial MOF-177. SF adsorption isotherms on commercial MOF-177 at three distinct temperatures (283.15, 298.15 and 328.15 K) were obtained by means of a gravimetric apparatus comprised of a McBain-type balance (detailed description has been reported elsewhere [20, 21]).
## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 shows the XRD pattern of as-received commercial MOF-177 together with the corresponding pattern of a spent sample from SF adsorption tests and the theoretical pattern as calculated from the crystallographic information file (CIF) n. 230642 of the Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre (CCDC).
The pattern of the spent sample is in good agreement with the simulated pattern Not very surprisingly, the pattern of the as-received sample significantly moves away from both the simulated one and that obtained from the same material after adsorption experiments. Indeed, even though the details of the commercial synthesis procedure for MOF-177 are obviously unknown, it is well known that the XRD pattern of assynthesized MOF-177 is strongly influenced by the choice of the synthesis solvent [22] and, more generally, by the nature of guest molecules inside its pore structure (which can also leads to a completely amorphous material-like pattern [23]). In the case of the spent commercial MOF-177 sample, all possible guest molecules have been removed during pre-SF-adsorption degas procedures. Moreover, before moving the spent sample from the adsorption chamber to the diffractometer, an additional evacuation step (followed by a backfill with nitrogen) contributed to minimize the residual presence of SF molecules inside the sample.
Fig. 2 shows the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $77\mathrm{K}$ on commercial MOF-177 and its pore size distribution according to the HK model. The textural properties derived from the data in Fig. 2 are reported in Table 1.
The shape of the $\Nu_{2}$ isotherm shown in Fig. 2(a) is quite similar to what was originally reported by Chae et al. [16]. This isotherm revealed a Type I behavior indicating that the accessible void space is fully saturated with $\Nu_{2}$ molecules before $p/p_{\theta}$ reaches 0.2, with a total uptake of $1030{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ STP of $\Nu_{2}$ per gram of MOF. Applying the Gurvitch’s rule, the corresponding TPV was determined to be $1.59\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , similar to the result reported by Chae et al. [16]. As regards the SSA, Type I isotherms are always associated with monolayer coverage [16], so the Langmuir method should provide a more reliable value of SSA than the BET method. The manufacturer of Basolite $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ Z377 reports a range of values for both Langmuir and BET SSAs [24], so they were both obtained from the data in Fig.2(a) (i.e., 5013 and $3948\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively). Eventually, the HK PSD shown in Fig. 2(b) is in good agreement with the theoretical pore structure of MOF-177 [16] indicating an almost unimodal distribution, with the maximum corresponding to the narrowest dimension $\mathrm{1.08\nm},$ ) of the theoretical pore structure. Moreover, a distribution peak around $1.15\mathrm{nm}$ (i.e., approximately the theoretical size of MOF-177 secondary cages) is clearly convoluted with the main one. To sum up, all reported characterization results confirmed that the purchased commercial product is made of a single crystalline phase corresponding to MOF-177.
Once the textural properties of commercial MOF-177 were verified, SF adsorption isotherm measurements were carried out at three distinct temperatures (283.15, 298.15 and 328.15 K) and the obtained results are shown in Fig. 3.
As expected, the commercial MOF-177 exhibited a very high SF adsorption capacity due to the high specific surface area as shown in Table 1. In particular, the SF adsorption capacity of commercial MOF-177 is also higher than that of MIL-101, which reportedly outperforms reference adsorbents for HGAGs [10]. Such observation should obviously be deepened in the light of competitive adsorption versus exhaled breatheconstituting compounds such as $\Nu_{2}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and, most importantly, water vapor [11, 12]. Moreover, commercial MOF-177 reaches its SF adsorption saturation capacity at very low pressures, e.g., below $7\mathrm{kPa}$ at $328.15\mathrm{K}$ and below $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{kPa}}$ at the lower temperatures (283.15 and 298.15 K), as shown in Fig. 3, thus suggesting a high adsorbent / adsorbate affinity. Unfortunately, due to the insufficient instrument accuracy of our in-house made experimental apparatus (i.e., dosing the adsorptive in the adsorption chamber at a pressure resolution of 0.1 kPa/dose is not feasible), it was not possible to acquire more reliable data collection along the steepest segment of the reported isotherms. Nonetheless, a very interesting observation can be made from the low-pressure blow up on the insert shown in Fig. 3, indicating that all reported
isotherms show a “kink” at a specific pressure, which increases with increasing equilibrium temperature. Such generally uncommon isotherm shape is only typical of a specific class of adsorbate / adsorbent pairs, e.g., water vapor adsorption on activated carbons. In 1996, Talu and Meunier [25] proposed a phenomenological model, together with a theoretical isotherm equation, which effectively describes this behavior. Following their theory, adsorption occurs in three steps, in the order of increased adsorbent loading (i.e., of adsorptive partial pressure): (1) primary adsorption occurs on a small fraction of the adsorbent surface; (2) the adsorbate molecules develop various shape clusters around primarily adsorbed molecules by association; (3) adsorption space is limited by micropore volume, regardless of the shape and/or radius of the pores. Such adsorption mechanism can be a priori considered plausible for SF adsorption on MOF177 in the light, on the one hand, of the size of SF molecules and, on the other hand, of the pore sizes of MOF-177. Indeed, SF has a critical diameter equal to $0.71{\mathrm{~nm}}$ [26], which should allow to easily accommodate one SF molecule in each MOF-177 cage. Moreover, MOF-177 primary cages are connected to produce continuous sinuous channels that allows free internal movement of molecules as big as Nile Red (molecular dimensions: $0.39\times0.94\times1.68\mathrm{nm}$ ) [16, 27], thus providing enough space for SF clustering starting from primarily adsorbed molecules. In order to check if this model can successfully describe SF adsorption on commercial MOF-177, we fit the experimental data, shown in Fig. 3, with the Talu-Meunier (TM) theoretical isotherm. This thermodynamically consistent equation represents adsorption equilibrium by three main parameters: the Henry’s law constant, the adsorbent saturation capacity and a reaction constant for cluster formation in micropores. Representing the SF equilibrium pressure as $p$ , the TM equation takes the following form:
$$
p={\frac{H\psi}{1+K\psi}}e x p{\left({\frac{\psi}{q_{m a x}}}\right)}
$$
In Eq. (1), $H$ is the Henry’s law constant, $q_{m a x}$ is the adsorbent saturation capacity, K is the reaction constant for cluster formation in micropores and ψ is related to SF adsorbed amount $q$ through the following two equations:
$$
\psi=\frac{-1+\sqrt{1+4K\xi}}{2K}
$$
$$
\xi=\frac{q_{m a x}\cdot q}{q_{m a x}-q}
$$
Temperature dependence of H and K can be expressed by the following relations:
$$
K=K_{0}e x p{\left[\frac{K_{1}}{T_{0}}\left(1-\frac{T_{0}}{T}\right)\right]}
$$
Eqs. (4) and (5) can be obtained by rewriting the exponential relations proposed by Talu and Meunier [25] with respect to a reference temperature $T_{0}$ . It should be noted that $q_{m a x}$ is considered temperature independent. However, in our case, the change of the adsorption saturation capacity with temperature is clearly observable from a simple inspection of experimental data in Fig. 3. For this reason, and considering that the
choice of the temperature dependence of $q_{m a x}$ can be quite arbitrary [28], the following linear expression is suggested to be used in connection with Eqs. (1-5):
$$
\begin{array}{r}{q_{m a x}=q_{m a x,0}+q_{v a r}\Big(1-\frac{T}{T_{0}}\Big)}\end{array}
$$
In Eq. (6), $q_{\nu a r}$ can be considered the portion of the total adsorption capacity that linearly varies with temperature with respect to a reference value ${\widehat{q_{m a x,0}}},$ which is temperature independent. The experimental data from Fig. 3 were then fitted by nonlinear regression employing the Origin $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ Fitting Function Organizer and simultaneously computing the best values of model parameters, e.g., $q_{m a x,o},q_{\nu a r},H_{0},H_{I},K_{O}$ and $K_{I}$ . The values of such parameters, by using $T_{0}{=}298.15\mathrm{K}$ as a reference temperature, are summarized in Table 2. The comparison between model fitting curves and experimental points is illustrated in Fig. 3.
Inspection of Fig. 3 clearly reveals that the theoretical curves fit experimental points very well, as indicated by the high value of the coefficient of determination $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ shown in Table 2. Moreover, the experimental values of the adsorption saturation capacity and the Henry’s law constant are very similar to those calculated by model fitting (see Table 3).
This is a confirmation that clustering of SF adsorbed molecules by self-association inside micropores provides a conceivable explanation for the adsorption of such HGAG on commercial MOF-177.
