## 4. Conclusions
This work highlighted a very peculiar equilibrium behavior of sevoflurane adsorption on commercial MOF-177. All isotherms reported in this study show a “kink” at a specific pressure, thus deviating from typical Type I isotherms, which becomes more evident with increasing equilibrium temperature. This behavior was hypothesized to depend on clustering of adsorbate molecules inside adsorbent micropores. Until now, this mechanism has been considered common only for water vapor adsorption on activated carbon. Indeed, an adsorption equilibrium model specific for the latter adsorbent / adsorbate couple (i.e., the Talu-Meunier theoretical isotherm) was found to excellently fit also the experimental results reported in this work. These findings
significantly deviate from what previously observed for HGAG adsorption on MOFs [10-12] and suggest a correlation between adsorption behavior and micropore structure (e.g., presence / absence of exposable CUSs, more / less uniform PSD). Eventually, the confirmation of such a correlation would provide essential information when designing an adsorption system with MOFs being used for capturing exhaled anesthetics.
